WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.566 --> 00:00:06.119
A camera films a drone show; a filter turns each drone into a white blob.

2
00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:09.694
Count them all, every frame: thirty-three milliseconds.

3
00:00:09.714 --> 00:00:13.746
On frames this small, compiled CPU code keeps up.

4
00:00:13.766 --> 00:00:18.354
Scale up: eighty-one megapixels, same thirty-three milliseconds.

5
00:00:18.374 --> 00:00:21.399
The CPU needs over a fifth of a second.

6
00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:23.671
Where does the GPU win?

7
00:00:23.691 --> 00:00:26.564
Two places: size and count.

8
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.320
Freeze.

9
00:00:28.875 --> 00:00:31.525
Zoom into one drone until its pixels show.

10
00:00:33.014 --> 00:00:37.426
On the CPU: paint a pixel, queue its red neighbours, repeat.

11
00:00:37.446 --> 00:00:42.059
One pixel per step, on one core, while twenty-four GPU SMs sit idle.

12
00:00:42.759 --> 00:00:46.434
So move it onto one SM, its queue in the SM's shared memory:

13
00:00:46.506 --> 00:00:48.586
two hundred fifty-six workers, called threads,

14
00:00:48.606 --> 00:00:51.409
in one block, paint a whole ring at once.

15
00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:55.069
But on one small blob, the CPU still wins.

16
00:00:55.089 --> 00:00:57.299
So: size first.

17
00:00:58.478 --> 00:01:01.198
On one giant blob, that queue overflows:

18
00:01:01.218 --> 00:01:04.273
a block gets only forty-eight kilobytes of shared memory,

19
00:01:04.293 --> 00:01:06.871
and this queue needs over a hundred.

20
00:01:06.891 --> 00:01:11.448
So the overflow spills to global memory: the card's eight gigabytes.

21
00:01:12.203 --> 00:01:14.928
Now one block is a little faster than the CPU.

22
00:01:15.538 --> 00:01:17.913
But that's one SM of twenty-four.

23
00:01:18.719 --> 00:01:21.602
So add a second block on a second SM.

24
00:01:21.622 --> 00:01:26.344
Squeezed onto one SM, two blocks ran one point seven times slower.

25
00:01:26.364 --> 00:01:29.347
But shared memory is private to its block.

26
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:31.819
So the whole queue moves to global memory:

27
00:01:31.839 --> 00:01:34.824
every new pixel's address is written there.

28
00:01:34.844 --> 00:01:38.042
On big blobs, measured, that's no slower.

29
00:01:38.062 --> 00:01:40.569
Two blocks are twice as fast.

30
00:01:40.589 --> 00:01:42.939
But twenty-two SMs stay dark.

31
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:47.860
So use every SM: forty-eight blocks, two on each.

32
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.548
Eight and a half times the CPU.

33
00:01:50.568 --> 00:01:52.275
Each colour is one block:

34
00:01:52.295 --> 00:01:57.208
they share one queue, so their pixels mix, and small rings keep only a few busy.

35
00:01:57.228 --> 00:02:00.263
We also tried other threads per block and block counts:

36
00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:03.798
on a smaller square, the best gained fifteen percent.

37
00:02:03.818 --> 00:02:06.543
We kept forty-eight by two hundred fifty-six.

38
00:02:07.236 --> 00:02:10.673
But on a thin snake, forty-eight blocks lose to one.

39
00:02:10.693 --> 00:02:12.091
Why?

40
00:02:12.111 --> 00:02:15.886
Every ring, the chip stops twice at a gate, two microseconds each.

41
00:02:16.647 --> 00:02:20.482
So pass fewer gates: step diagonally too.

42
00:02:20.502 --> 00:02:22.952
On a square, half the rings, half the gates.

43
00:02:24.205 --> 00:02:26.032
Size is won.

44
00:02:26.052 --> 00:02:30.895
Now count: twenty-five hundred blobs in the big frame, one launch each.

45
00:02:30.915 --> 00:02:34.750
Slower than the CPU: we built for size, not count.

46
00:02:35.501 --> 00:02:37.726
So tag each queue entry with its blob:

47
00:02:37.886 --> 00:02:41.228
one spare bit of the entry's word, in global memory.

48
00:02:41.248 --> 00:02:46.323
Each new pixel inherits the tag, and the tag picks its colour: its label.

49
00:02:46.343 --> 00:02:50.646
One bit tells two blobs apart: two per launch, faster.

50
00:02:50.666 --> 00:02:55.206
But still slower than the CPU: only two seeds, each handed in.

51
00:02:55.904 --> 00:02:58.054
So the GPU finds its own seeds:

52
00:02:58.194 --> 00:03:02.051
every blob in one fill launch, about fifty milliseconds.

53
00:03:02.071 --> 00:03:04.269
Faster than the CPU.

54
00:03:04.289 --> 00:03:08.134
But over budget: every red pixel still rides the queue.

55
00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:14.032
So the queue carries tiles, not pixels: squares of thirty-two by thirty-two.

56
00:03:14.052 --> 00:03:17.815
One warp, thirty-two threads that run in step, takes a tile.

57
00:03:17.835 --> 00:03:21.010
Each thread holds one row, one bit per pixel.

58
00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:26.202
The row sits in registers: the thread's own memory, closer than shared memory.

59
00:03:26.222 --> 00:03:28.652
Fill along every row at once, step diagonally,

60
00:03:28.672 --> 00:03:32.060
fill along the columns, and repeat until nothing changes.

61
00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:36.042
Only the tile's border bits go out, into the tiles around it,

62
00:03:36.062 --> 00:03:38.237
and those join the next round's queue.

63
00:03:38.875 --> 00:03:41.812
One gate per round, not two per ring.

64
00:03:41.832 --> 00:03:46.152
On the giant square, four thousand rings become a hundred twenty-seven rounds:

65
00:03:46.172 --> 00:03:48.852
from a hundred milliseconds to three.

66
00:03:48.872 --> 00:03:51.397
But that's one blob, from one seed.

67
00:03:52.566 --> 00:03:57.534
So first, every tile finds the blob pieces inside it, in registers, all at once.

68
00:03:57.554 --> 00:03:59.266
Some pieces are seeds:

69
00:03:59.286 --> 00:04:03.591
each seed sends a wave from piece to piece, one round at a time.

70
00:04:03.611 --> 00:04:05.841
Where two waves meet, the meeting is noted,

71
00:04:05.861 --> 00:04:09.036
and at the end each blob keeps its smallest label.

72
00:04:09.712 --> 00:04:15.140
On the big frame: over eleven thousand seeds, six rounds, every blob counted.

73
00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.932
Two point two milliseconds, once the frame is on the card: inside the budget.

74
00:04:21.713 --> 00:04:25.528
One huge blob, or many at once: the GPU.

75
00:04:25.548 --> 00:04:27.798
And one small blob?

76
00:04:27.818 --> 00:04:33.416
In kernel time, the GPU now; with the copy to the card, still the CPU.

77
00:04:33.436 --> 00:04:35.498
Every drone, every frame.
